<div class="page-header">
  <div class="container">
    <h1 class="page-title">Sky over Ahvantir</h1>
    <p class="page-lead">Solara, Nystara and the three moons placed for any hour of any day, seen from Aru&rsquo;Mas. Drag the sky to turn, scrub the hour, or set it running.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="sky">

  {# Same pattern as the almanac ribbon and the relationship graph: a canvas
     cannot be navigated by tabbing through it, so it is one tab stop that
     listens for keys, and the keys are written down where a reader will find
     them rather than left to be guessed. #}
  <p class="alm-hint" id="sky-keys">
    Tab to the sky, then use the left and right arrows to turn, the up and down
    arrows to move the clock by a quarter hour, Page&nbsp;Up and Page&nbsp;Down to
    change the day, Home to face north, and Space to start or stop. Plus and minus
    zoom and 0 returns to the full horizon; once you are zoomed in, Shift with the
    up and down arrows aims higher or lower, and dragging does both at once. Shift
    with left or right turns further. Every control below is reachable on its own,
    and everything the sky itself can open also has a link under the ephemeris.
  </p>

  <section class="sky-stage" aria-labelledby="sky-stage-head">
    <h2 class="sr-only" id="sky-stage-head">The sky at the selected moment</h2>
    <div class="sky-box" id="sky-box">
      <canvas id="sky-canvas" class="sky-canvas" tabindex="0" role="application"
              aria-describedby="sky-keys"
              aria-label="The sky over Aru'Mas at the selected day and hour"></canvas>
      <div class="sky-hud">
        <div class="sky-hud__date" id="sky-hud-date"></div>
        <div class="sky-hud__state" id="sky-hud-state"></div>
        <div class="sky-hud__state" id="sky-hud-fov"></div>
        <div class="sky-hud__state sky-hud__at" id="sky-hud-at"></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    {# The view buttons live in the controls, not over the canvas. On a phone
       there is no room for them beside the date and they sat on top of it; a
       wide screen has the room, so a media query lifts them back over the
       top-right corner of the sky. DOM order stays the same either way, which
       keeps the tab order sensible. #}
    <div class="sky-controls">
      <div class="sky-viewbtns">
        <button type="button" id="sky-view-pan" aria-pressed="true">Horizon</button>
        <button type="button" id="sky-view-dome" aria-pressed="false">Dome</button>
        <button type="button" id="sky-face-n" aria-label="Face north">N</button>
        <button type="button" id="sky-face-e" aria-label="Face east">E</button>
        <button type="button" id="sky-face-s" aria-label="Face south">S</button>
        <button type="button" id="sky-face-w" aria-label="Face west">W</button>
      </div>

      <div class="sky-row">
        <div class="sky-field">
          <label for="sky-year">Year</label>
          <button type="button" class="map-btn" id="sky-prev-year" aria-label="Previous year">&lsaquo;</button>
          <input id="sky-year" class="sky-ctl" type="number" min="0" max="9999" value="439" inputmode="numeric">
          <button type="button" class="map-btn" id="sky-next-year" aria-label="Next year">&rsaquo;</button>
          <span class="sky-num">MC</span>
        </div>
        <div class="sky-field">
          <label for="sky-month">Month</label>
          <select class="sky-ctl" id="sky-month"></select>
        </div>
        <div class="sky-field">
          <label for="sky-day">Day</label>
          <select class="sky-ctl" id="sky-day"></select>
        </div>
        <div class="sky-field">
          <button type="button" class="map-btn" id="sky-prev-day">&lsaquo; Day</button>
          <button type="button" class="map-btn" id="sky-next-day">Day &rsaquo;</button>
        </div>
        <div class="sky-field sky-field--right">
          <button type="button" class="map-btn" id="sky-play" aria-pressed="false">Play</button>
          <label for="sky-speed">Speed</label>
          <select class="sky-ctl" id="sky-speed">
            <option value="10">10 min / sec</option>
            <option value="60" selected>1 hour / sec</option>
            <option value="day">1 day / sec</option>
          </select>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="sky-timeline">
        <span class="sky-clock" id="sky-clock">00:00</span>
        <div class="sky-slider">
          <div class="sky-band" id="sky-band" aria-hidden="true"></div>
          <input id="sky-hour" class="sky-hour" type="range" min="0" max="23.983" step="0.0167"
                 aria-label="Hour of day">
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="sky-ticks" aria-hidden="true">
        <span>00</span><span>03</span><span>06</span><span>09</span><span>12</span>
        <span>15</span><span>18</span><span>21</span><span>24</span>
      </div>
      <p class="sr-only" id="sky-status" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true"></p>
    </div>

    {# Everything above is about WHEN. This strip is about WHAT you are looking
       at: a way to find a thing by name, and the three switches that change
       what the sky draws. They used to sit at the bottom of the modelling
       notes, which is the right place for the dials that change the model and
       the wrong place for the ones that change the view. #}
    <div class="sky-controls sky-controls--find">
      <div class="sky-field sky-field--find">
        <label for="sky-search">Find</label>
        <input id="sky-search" class="sky-ctl" type="text" list="sky-search-list"
               placeholder="a figure, a body, a sign or a month"
               autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false">
        <datalist id="sky-search-list"></datalist>
        <button type="button" class="map-btn" id="sky-search-go">Go</button>
      </div>
      <span class="sky-field"><label for="sky-fignames"
            title="Name each figure on the sky itself, not only in the list below.">Figure names</label><input id="sky-fignames" type="checkbox" checked></span>
      <span class="sky-field"><label for="sky-sunpath"
            title="The path the suns walk, with a tick at each of the thirteen signs.">The suns&rsquo; road</label><input id="sky-sunpath" type="checkbox"></span>
      <span class="sky-field"><label for="sky-coords"
            title="Read out where the pointer is: height, bearing, and the sign it falls in.">Pointer position</label><input id="sky-coords" type="checkbox"></span>
      <span class="sky-field"><label for="sky-big"
            title="Draw the discs three times life size so they are visible at all. Gaps are stretched to match, so an overlap on screen is a real one.">Magnify discs &times;3</label><input id="sky-big" type="checkbox" checked></span>
      <p class="sky-find-note alm-note" id="sky-search-note" aria-live="polite"></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sky-grid">
    <div class="alm-panel">
      <div>
        <p class="alm-eyebrow">Ephemeris for the day</p>
        <h2 class="alm-dayhead__title" id="sky-eph-head"></h2>
      </div>
      <div class="sky-pills" id="sky-pills"></div>
      <div class="sky-tablewrap">
        <table class="sky-table">
          <caption class="sr-only">Where each body stands now, when it rises and sets, and its phase</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Body</th><th scope="col">Now</th><th scope="col">In</th><th scope="col">Rises</th><th scope="col">Sets</th><th scope="col">Phase</th><th scope="col">Brightness</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody id="sky-eph"></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="alm-eyebrow">Apparent size, right now</p>
        <div class="sky-sizes" id="sky-sizes" role="img" aria-labelledby="sky-sizes-desc"></div>
        <p class="sr-only" id="sky-sizes-desc">The five bodies drawn in proportion to how wide each looks from the ground at this moment.</p>
        <p class="alm-note">Drawn to the same scale as each other, so the comparison holds even when the sky above is magnified. The suns change size through the year as Ahvantir's orbit carries it nearer and further; Nystara swings widest, sitting furthest from the centre the pair turns about.</p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <p class="alm-eyebrow">Figures overhead</p>
        <ul class="sky-figures" id="sky-figures"></ul>
        <p class="alm-note">Click a figure in the sky, or any sun or moon, to read about it. A figure appears only once the whole of it has cleared the horizon.</p>
      </div>

      <p class="alm-note">Moon phase names follow the Foundry phase tables, so they match the <a href="/almanac/" class="wikilink">Sky Almanac</a> and the table&rsquo;s own moon widget. The Pale Hour is Nystara in the sky with Solara below it &mdash; named for the light, not the length, so it can run to minutes or to most of two hours. Solara alone is simply the warm light, which has no other name.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="alm-panel sky-dialwrap">
      <div style="align-self: stretch">
        <p class="alm-eyebrow">Gnomon at the selected hour</p>
        <h2 class="alm-dayhead__title">Two Shadows</h2>
      </div>
      <canvas id="sky-dial" class="sky-dial" width="520" height="520"
              aria-label="A post and the two shadows it casts"></canvas>
      <p class="alm-note" id="sky-dial-note" style="align-self: stretch" aria-live="polite"></p>
      <p class="alm-note" style="align-self: stretch">Every lit object in Ahvantir casts two shadows &mdash; one warm and soft from Solara, one sharper and edged toward pale blue from Nystara. Where they overlap the shade is deeper and cooler than either alone.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <details class="sky-model">
    <summary>How this sky is modelled</summary>
    <div class="sky-model__body">
      <p><strong>From canon.</strong> Solara is the larger, warm yellow disc; Nystara is smaller, blue-white and hotter. They orbit their common centre, and Nyhexus orbits them both &mdash; which is why the pair closes and opens once a year rather than once a decade, and why the two do, now and then, cross. Moon cycles, phase tables and the Year&nbsp;1 moon epoch come from the Foundry calendar, and the day runs to 24 hours as Foundry runs it. The constellations and their names are the DM&rsquo;s.</p>

      <p><strong>What follows from the geometry, rather than being chosen.</strong> The suns converge once a year, because the planet carries you right around them in that time. How close each Pairing gets rides a rhythm of about eight years, as the pair&rsquo;s own orbit turns underneath the annual one. Twice a year the planet crosses the plane the suns orbit in and sees them exactly edge-on; when a deep Pairing lands on one of those crossings, one sun passes in front of the other.</p>
      <p>Four more fell out of the same arithmetic and were not aimed at:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Across twelve centuries, <strong>every close Pairing falls in Ossandrel or Lochenvir</strong> and in no other month. A close Pairing needs the planet near a node of the suns&rsquo; plane, and those two crossings land at fixed points in the year.</li>
        <li>Both suns are bitten at once about <strong>once a century</strong>, and about half of those are visible from Aru&rsquo;Mas &mdash; but in twelve hundred years <strong>never both wholly covered</strong>. The sky cannot be put out by eclipse.</li>
        <li>The suns stand up to 18.5&deg; apart, which is most of a sign, so the two are <strong>routinely in different signs</strong>. Any astrology built on this sky has to say which sun it means.</li>
        <li>A <em>total</em> crossing of Nystara &mdash; the most dramatic thing this sky does, the smaller sun swallowed whole &mdash; takes <strong>less than a fifth of the daylight</strong> with it. Its disc is only that much of the pair&rsquo;s combined face.</li>
      </ul>

      <p><strong>Working assumptions.</strong> These are invented to make a picture and are not settled lore. The first three are adjustable here so you can see what they change:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>The observer stands in Aru&rsquo;Mas at the latitude below. Day length and how high the suns climb both depend on it.</li>
        <li>The longest day falls in mid-Cindralis and the longest night on Noctharis, matching the month descriptions.</li>
        <li>The suns stand at most about 18.5&deg; apart, which follows from the planet orbiting at a little over three times their own separation. That in turn gives a longest Pale Hour of just over an hour and three quarters &mdash; the &ldquo;nearly two hours&rdquo; the lore describes.</li>
        <li>The suns orbit each other in 16.37 years &mdash; deliberately not a whole number of years. At exactly sixteen, every Pairing would land on a plane crossing and every one would be a flawless eclipse; the slight mismatch is what keeps eclipses rare.</li>
        <li>The planet&rsquo;s orbit is tilted 45&deg; to the suns&rsquo;. This is the one number picked for feel rather than derived, and it is the dial below: it sets how often an eclipse happens, from every other year at a few degrees to once a generation near ninety.</li>
        <li>Expect short Pale Hours around a close Pairing &mdash; when the suns reach the horizon almost together, it shrinks to minutes. It is named for its light rather than its length, so a short one is still a Pale Hour.</li>
        <li>Moon sizes and orbital tilts are invented, so a moon seen crossing a sun&rsquo;s face here is a result of these numbers and not a lore event.</li>
        <li><strong>Nothing in canon divides the suns&rsquo; road.</strong> The thirteen signs, and where they begin, are a working construction. The first is anchored at right ascension 178&deg; so that the merging of 0&nbsp;MC falls inside The Spear&rsquo;s sign; move that anchor and every figure&rsquo;s sign moves with it. The numbers are labels, not a tradition anyone in the world uses.</li>
        <li><strong>The pole star was placed, not found.</strong> Before it the axis was marked by a dim star sixteen degrees adrift, and north could not be had by looking. It now sits a degree and a half off the pole, so it circles a small ring each night and its height above the horizon is your latitude.</li>
        <li>The <strong>faint layer is drawn for this page</strong> and is not lore &mdash; it is there so the sky looks like a sky. The named stars are a fixed catalogue that the figures point into, and the figures&rsquo; shapes are still drafts.</li>
        <li><strong>Brightness</strong> is counted the way star charts count it: lower is brighter, each step about two and a half times the light. Two anchors fix the scale &mdash; the faintest star a sharp eye finds on a dark night at 6, and the brightest star in the sky at &minus;1.5. The crystal glasses of the Arcane Observatory are taken to reach three magnitudes deeper, to 9. One consequence is worth stating plainly: no named star here is fainter than magnitude 2.1, which makes this sky about three times as bright as Earth&rsquo;s. That is on purpose.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="sky-model__ctl">
        <span class="sky-field"><label for="sky-lat">Latitude</label><input class="sky-ctl" id="sky-lat" type="number" min="-80" max="80" step="1">&deg;</span>
        <span class="sky-field"><label for="sky-tilt">Axial tilt</label><input class="sky-ctl" id="sky-tilt" type="number" min="0" max="60" step="1">&deg;</span>
        <span class="sky-field"><label for="sky-spread">Orbit tilt</label><input class="sky-ctl" id="sky-spread" type="number" min="0" max="89" step="1">&deg;</span>
      </div>
      <p class="alm-note">Across the whole horizon a sun is about four pixels wide, so the discs are drawn at three times their real size to be visible at all &mdash; and the gaps between them are stretched by the same amount, close in, so that <strong>two discs touch on screen exactly when they truly touch</strong>. Without that the picture would show transits the day&rsquo;s event list denies. Nothing is moved by more than about a degree, and only ever beside something it is nearly touching. Turn magnifying off and zoom in &mdash; scroll on the sky, or use plus and minus &mdash; and everything is at true angular size. The strip under the ephemeris always shows the real proportions whatever the view is doing.</p>
      <div class="sky-field">
        <button type="button" class="map-btn" id="sky-share">Link to this sky</button>
        <input class="sky-share-out" id="sky-share-out" type="text" readonly hidden
               aria-label="Link to the sky currently shown">
      </div>
    </div>
  </details>

  <section class="alm-notes" aria-labelledby="sky-notes-head">
    <p class="alm-eyebrow">Where this comes from</p>
    <h2 id="sky-notes-head">Sources</h2>
    <p>The calendar is the <a href="/articles/marducian-calendar/" class="wikilink">Marducian Calendar</a>; the suns, the Pairing, the two shadows and the Pale Hour are all from <a href="/articles/solara-and-nystara/" class="wikilink">Solara and Nystara</a>. For the year as a whole &mdash; every day&rsquo;s moon phases, the convergences and the dark skies &mdash; see the <a href="/almanac/" class="wikilink">Sky Almanac</a>.</p>
  </section>

